# Vantora AI — environment template
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in what you need. Two values are REQUIRED to sign in
# and approve anything; everything else is optional and degrades honestly:
#
#   REQUIRED  BETTER_AUTH_SECRET   without it every page and API answers
#                                  "authentication is not configured" — there
#                                  is no bypass and no anonymous mode.
#   REQUIRED  HITL_SIGNING_SECRET  signs the approval tokens the browser carries
#                                  from "approve" to "execute". Without it Tier 2
#                                  operations fail closed.
#
#   optional  GEMINI_API_KEY       without it the copilot answers from the
#                                  deterministic synthesiser and says so.
#   optional  QUANT_ENGINE_URL     without the Python engine the seeded
#                                  TypeScript mirror serves the numbers.
#   optional  DATABASE_URL         without it the ledger is the in-memory
#                                  synthetic dataset. (Accounts, approvals and
#                                  executions always live in the local SQLite
#                                  file below, independent of this.)
#
# Generate secrets with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
# Secrets in this file are server-only. None is ever sent to the browser.

# ─── Authentication (REQUIRED) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Better Auth: email + password, scrypt-hashed, sessions in data/vantora.db.
# 32+ random characters. Rotating it signs every user out.
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET="replace-with-32+-random-characters"
#
# The public origin the app is served from. Cookies are marked Secure when this
# is https://. Cross-origin POSTs to the API are refused against it.
# BETTER_AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"
#
# Accounts are created only by the owner, on the server — there is no sign-up:
#   npm run users -- bootstrap-owner --email owner@example.com --name "Plant Owner"
#   npm run users -- add --email analyst@example.com --name "Analyst" --role ANALYST
#   npm run users -- add --email viewer@example.com --name "Viewer" --role VIEWER
# A one-time password is printed unless VANTORA_NEW_USER_PASSWORD is set.

# ─── Approval signing (REQUIRED to approve) ───────────────────────────────────
# HMAC-SHA256 secret for Tier 2 approval tokens. 32+ random characters.
# HITL_SIGNING_SECRET="replace-with-32+-random-characters"

# ─── App database (optional override) ────────────────────────────────────────
# SQLite file for users, sessions, roles, approvals and sandbox executions.
# VANTORA_DB_PATH="./data/vantora.db"
#
# Application audit chain (JSONL). Tests always use a temporary file instead.
# VANTORA_AUDIT_LOG="./logs/sandbox_operation_logs.jsonl"

# ─── Copilot model provider (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────
# AI_PROVIDER picks who answers: "gemini" (default when unset) or "openrouter".
# Only the selected provider's settings are checked; the other key may stay
# empty. With a working provider the copilot runs a bounded tool-calling loop
# over Vantora's gated tools. Without one, or when the provider fails, the
# deterministic synthesiser answers and the UI labels it as a fallback — the app
# never switches to the other provider on its own. Restart the server after
# changing any of these. Keys stay server-side: never prefix them NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# AI_PROVIDER="gemini"

# Google Gemini API (AI_PROVIDER=gemini)
# GEMINI_API_KEY="your-gemini-api-key"
# GEMINI_MODEL="gemini-3.1-flash-lite"            # default when unset

# OpenRouter (AI_PROVIDER=openrouter). OPENROUTER_MODEL may be any OpenRouter
# model that supports tool calling; one that does not is reported as
# "model_incompatible" and the fallback answers.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY="your-openrouter-api-key"
# OPENROUTER_MODEL="z-ai/glm-5.3-flash"           # default when unset
# OPENROUTER_BASE_URL="https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"  # default when unset

# ─── Python quantitative engine (optional) ────────────────────────────────────
# Where the FastAPI MILP/Monte Carlo service listens. Start it with
# `npm run quant`, or both servers with `npm run dev:all`.
# QUANT_ENGINE_URL="http://127.0.0.1:8000"

# ─── Ledger database (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Supabase PostgreSQL for the synthetic ledger tables and an AuditLog mirror.
# Then run `npm run db:push`. Prisma 7 reads these through prisma.config.ts.
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true"
# DIRECT_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/postgres"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co"
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY="sb_publishable_..."
